/// \defgroup grp_w1_script_templates Script action and condition templates
/// \ingroup grp_scripting
/// \brief The built-in catalogue of map-script action and condition types:
///        internal names, editor menu paths, sentence text and parameter
///        types.
///
/// Every action and condition in a map script is stored as a type number.
/// `ScriptEngine` keeps one `Template` record per type, one table for
/// actions and one for conditions, and fills both in code during
/// ScriptEngine::init. The records tell the rest of the game how many
/// parameters a type takes and of what kind, which internal name identifies
/// the type in map files and INI (and, in BFME 1, in Lua), and how the type
/// reads in editors. The behaviour of each type lives elsewhere: in
/// `ScriptActions` and `ScriptConditions`, in ScriptEngine::executeActions
/// and ScriptEngine::evaluateCondition (which handle a few core types
/// themselves), and, for the wait-type actions of sequential scripts, in
/// ScriptEngine::evaluateAndProgressAllSequentialScripts.
/// \ref sub_scripting describes the script engine as a whole.


/// \class Template
/// \ingroup grp_w1_script_templates
/// \brief Description of one map-script action or condition type.
///
/// `ScriptEngine` owns two fixed arrays of these records. A record's index
/// is the type number that ScriptAction and Condition objects store. The
/// records are filled once at startup and live as long as the engine; they
/// are looked up with ScriptEngine::getActionTemplate and
/// ScriptEngine::getConditionTemplate, which return entry 0 for a type out
/// of range.
///
/// A record holds, by role:
/// - **Mode mask** (BFME 2 only). ScriptEngine::evaluateCondition treats a
///   condition as false, ScriptEngine::executeActions skips an action, and
///   ScriptEngine::evaluateAndProgressAllSequentialScripts skips such an
///   action in a sequential script, when the template's mask shares no bit
///   with the engine's current mode. The mode is 2 when a GameLogic test on
///   the current game mode is true and 1 otherwise; which game modes that
///   test accepts is not established.
///   The default mask is 1. The builders set 3 (both modes) on 16 actions
///   and 5 conditions drawn from the core scripting types (for example
///   `SET_FLAG`, `SET_COUNTER`, `NO_OP`, `ENABLE_SCRIPT`,
///   `CONDITION_TRUE`). Other scripting types, such as the timer actions,
///   keep 1. They set 2 on only six Living World types (five actions and
///   `LIVING_WORLD_IS_REGION_REF_BOUND`), while other `LIVING_WORLD_`
///   actions such as `LIVING_WORLD_SPAWN_ARMY_IN_ZONE` keep 1. That
///   suggests mode 2 belongs to the Living World, but it is an inference,
///   not confirmed.
/// - **UI name**: a slash-separated menu path such as
///   `Scripting_/Flags/Set flag to value.`.
///   ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys appends the type number in square
///   brackets. Zero Hour's WorldBuilder files each type in its picker tree by
///   this path.
/// - **Second UI name**: an optional second menu path (Zero Hour's
///   WorldBuilder lists the type under both). The BFME 2 builders leave it
///   empty.
/// - **Internal name**, such as `SET_FLAG`, and its cached name key. The
///   internal name is the identifier that map files (as a name key) and INI
///   blocks use, and that BFME 1's Lua `ExecuteAction` and
///   `EvaluateCondition` look up.
/// - **UI strings**: up to twelve sentence fragments that alternate with the
///   parameters. Template::getUiStrings returns them, and
///   ScriptAction::getUiText and Condition::getUiText splice them with the
///   parameter values into a readable sentence.
/// - **Parameter count and types**: up to twelve parameter type values.
///   ScriptAction::setActionType and Condition::setConditionType create one
///   parameter of each listed type. Template::getParameterType returns the
///   integer type for an index past the count.
/// - **Help text**: empty unless INI supplies it.
///
/// ScriptEngine's constructor default-constructs every slot of both tables.
/// The record's default constructor sets the mode mask to 1, the UI name to
/// `UNUSED/(placeholder)/placeholder` (the same placeholder Zero Hour's
/// Template constructor uses), both counts to zero and every parameter type
/// to the integer type. A slot that the builders never fill keeps these
/// values.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The internal name is the stable key. From condition chunk version 4 a
///   map stores each condition's internal name key next to its type number.
///   Condition::ParseConditionDataChunk keeps the type when the keys agree,
///   otherwise searches the table for the name. It turns the condition into
///   `CONDITION_FALSE`, dropping its parameters, when the name is unknown or
///   the stored parameter count differs from the template's. In BFME 2 that
///   fallback also catches every condition from a chunk older than version
///   4, which stores no name key, so such conditions always load as
///   `CONDITION_FALSE`. Zero Hour applies the name fallback only to version
///   4 chunks.
///   WriteActionDataChunk stores the action's internal name the same way.
///   BFME 2's action reader has not been verified; in Zero Hour and BFME 1
///   it resolves the name the same way, and an unknown name becomes
///   `NO_OP`. Renaming an internal name therefore breaks every map that
///   uses it, and so does changing a condition's parameter count.
/// - INI can relabel a type but not create one. BFME 2's INI block table has
///   `ScriptAction` and `ScriptCondition` blocks. Both blocks share one field
///   table with the fields `InternalName` (used only to find the entry),
///   `UIName`, `UIName2` and `HelpText`, the same names as Zero Hour. The
///   handlers search from type 0 for the first entry whose internal name
///   matches, copy the UI name, second UI name and help text onto it, and
///   change nothing else. All three are copied, so a field the block omits
///   resets that text: an omitted `UIName` becomes
///   `UNUSED/(placeholder)/placeholder`, an omitted `UIName2` or `HelpText`
///   becomes empty. A block whose `InternalName` matches no entry is
///   ignored.
/// - BFME 2 registers the Lua functions `ExecuteAction` and
///   `EvaluateCondition`. In BFME 1 they find the template by exact,
///   case-sensitive internal name and require exactly the template's number
///   of parameters.
///
/// \note Zero Hour derives Template from MemoryPoolObject, which has a
///       virtual destructor, and uses empty subclasses ActionTemplate and
///       ConditionTemplate for the two tables. BFME 1's record starts with
///       the UI name. In BFME 2 the record starts with the mode mask, so it
///       has no virtual table at its start.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The names Template, ActionTemplate and ConditionTemplate and the field
/// roles come from the Zero Hour and BFME 1 sources. The BFME 2 ledger
/// carries them in the rows for Template::getUiStrings,
/// Template::getParameterType and the two table getters. The mode mask, its
/// readers, the INI field table and the default constructor's values are
/// BFME 2 target facts from matched bodies. Calling it a mode mask is an
/// inference from those readers.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates,
///      ScriptEngine::initConditionTemplates,
///      ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys, Template::getUiStrings,
///      Template::getParameterType


/// \class ScriptEngine
/// \ingroup grp_w1_script_templates
/// \brief The map-script engine, `TheScriptEngine`. This group covers only
///        its action and condition template tables.
///
/// The engine object embeds two fixed arrays of Template records: 599
/// action slots and 202 condition slots. ScriptEngine::init fills them once,
/// by calling ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates,
/// ScriptEngine::initConditionTemplates and
/// ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys in that order, before
/// ScriptEngine::reset. The tables are embedded in the engine and live as
/// long as it does. Apart from the start-up builders, the only known writers
/// are the INI `ScriptAction` and `ScriptCondition` handlers. They find the
/// entry by internal name and replace its UI name, second UI name and help
/// text (see Template).
///
/// Readers of the tables:
/// - ScriptEngine::getActionTemplate and ScriptEngine::getConditionTemplate,
///   which map a type out of range to entry 0;
/// - ScriptAction::setActionType and Condition::setConditionType, which
///   create a new action's or condition's parameters from its template;
/// - Condition::ParseConditionDataChunk and WriteActionDataChunk, which
///   check or store the internal name in map files;
/// - ScriptEngine::evaluateCondition, ScriptEngine::executeActions and
///   ScriptEngine::evaluateAndProgressAllSequentialScripts, which test the
///   mode mask;
/// - ScriptAction::getUiText and Condition::getUiText, which build readable
///   sentences. Script::getUiText calls both;
///   ScriptEngine::evaluateAndProgressAllSequentialScripts also calls
///   ScriptAction::getUiText.
///
/// \par Modding
/// Both tables are fixed-size arrays built in code. A new type needs a table
/// slot and a case in the code that carries it out, so data alone cannot add
/// one. Four action and seven condition slots are unused. Going beyond them
/// means enlarging the arrays embedded in ScriptEngine, and the 599/202
/// bounds are repeated as constants in the table getters,
/// ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys, the INI relabel searches and the
/// condition loader. A condition whose internal name the game does not know
/// loads as `CONDITION_FALSE`; in Zero Hour and BFME 1 such an action loads
/// as `NO_OP`.
///
/// \par Remastering
/// The tables are plain data with no platform dependency. Map compatibility
/// rests on the internal names: keeping them keeps maps loading (confirmed
/// for conditions in BFME 2; for actions in Zero Hour and BFME 1). The type
/// numbers are tied to the dispatch code and must stay in step with it (see
/// ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates). The retail BFME 2 WorldBuilder has
/// its own compiled copy of the action table (its strings match), so the
/// editor and the game have to change together.
///
/// \see Template, \ref sub_scripting


/// \fn void ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates()
/// \ingroup grp_w1_script_templates
/// \brief Fills the action template table: one record for every map-script
///        action type.
///
/// ScriptEngine::init calls this once during engine start-up, before
/// ScriptEngine::initConditionTemplates. The result is the complete list of
/// actions a map script can use. Each type gets its internal name (such as
/// `SET_FLAG` or `NAMED_ATTACK_AREA`), its menu path, its parameter types and
/// its sentence fragments. ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys then appends
/// each type number to the UI name and caches the internal name's key.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The body is one long run of assignments. It selects a record by type
/// number and sets its internal name, UI name, parameter count, parameter
/// types, UI-string count, UI strings and, for some types, the mode mask.
/// Records are not filled in type order; related types are grouped together
/// (debug, flags, counters and so on), as in the BFME 1 and Zero Hour
/// sources. Retail quirks, kept because the matched body reproduces them:
/// - Four slots are never filled and keep the placeholder name.
/// - The body also fills two condition records, `UNIT_THREAT_LEVEL` and
///   `TEAM_THREAT_LEVEL`, between two action records. Their addresses lie
///   past the end of the action table, inside the condition table, in two
///   slots that ScriptEngine::initConditionTemplates skips. The original
///   code most likely indexed the action table out of range (an inference);
///   Open-BFME-1's reconstruction of BFME 1 writes the same two records
///   through out-of-range action indices too. They keep the default mode
///   mask.
/// - `VICTORY` and `DEFEAT` are written twice. The later write gives them
///   the `Multiplayer_/` menu path instead of `User_/`; Zero Hour's table
///   has the same double entry. `CAMERA_FADE_MULTIPLY` is also written
///   twice.
/// - Two adjacent slots both carry the internal name
///   `MAP_REVEAL_IN_TRIGGER` with the same menu path and parameters, but
///   the second one's sentence says the map is *shrouded*. A search by name
///   that starts at type 0 (BFME 1's Lua `ExecuteAction`, or the INI
///   relabel handlers) always finds the first one, so the second cannot be
///   reached by name.
/// - `TEAM_ALL_USE_COMMANDBUTTON_ON_NAMED` lists three parameter types but a
///   count of two, so the third is never used. `CAMERA_BW_MODE_END` has a
///   count of one and no listed type, as in Zero Hour; the record
///   constructor zeroed the types, so its parameter is an integer.
/// - Mask 3 is set on 16 actions, all core scripting types (debug, flags,
///   counters, enabling and calling scripts, no-op), and mask 2 on five
///   `LIVING_WORLD_` actions. Every other action keeps 1, including the
///   timer actions and the other Living World actions.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - The parameter types decide what kind of value each argument holds and
///   what an editor offers for it. The UI strings only change how the action
///   reads.
/// - Do not renumber slots: the type number is the key of the dispatch
///   switch in ScriptEngine::executeActions (which tests literal type
///   numbers) and of other hard-coded uses, such as the wait-type tests in
///   ScriptEngine::evaluateAndProgressAllSequentialScripts; in Zero Hour,
///   the `ScriptActions` dispatcher also switches on it. WorldBuilder carries
///   its own copy of the table. Maps store both the number and the internal
///   name; whether BFME 2's action loader falls back to the name when the
///   two disagree is not confirmed. The table holds 599 action slots.
/// - `SET_COUNTER_TO_CLIENT_RANDOM_VALUE` reads the client random generator.
///   Its own UI text warns that it desyncs multiplayer games. Actions run
///   inside the deterministic game logic on every machine, so a new action
///   must change game state identically everywhere.
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The body follows BFME 1, where the same records are filled in the same
/// statement order inside BFME 1's ScriptEngine::init. Statements absent
/// from BFME 1 were read from the BFME 2 retail stores. No retail symbol
/// names this function; `initActionTemplates` is a descriptive name. Zero
/// Hour fills its smaller table inline in ScriptEngine::init.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::initConditionTemplates,
///      ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys, ScriptEngine::getActionTemplate,
///      ScriptAction::setActionType, ScriptEngine::executeActions


/// \fn void ScriptEngine::initConditionTemplates()
/// \ingroup grp_w1_script_templates
/// \brief Fills the condition template table: one record for every
///        map-script condition type.
///
/// ScriptEngine::init calls this once, after
/// ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates and before
/// ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys. It is the condition counterpart of
/// the action table. Type 0 is `CONDITION_FALSE`, which the map reader
/// substitutes for a condition it cannot resolve, and type 1 is `COUNTER`.
///
/// \par Implementation
/// The same assignment pattern as the action table: select a record by type
/// number, then set its internal name, UI name, parameter count and types,
/// UI strings and, for some types, the mode mask. Retail quirks:
/// - The builder skips nine slots, the same nine as BFME 1's condition
///   builder. Two of them hold `UNIT_THREAT_LEVEL` and `TEAM_THREAT_LEVEL`,
///   which ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates writes (see there); the other
///   seven are never filled and keep the placeholder name.
/// - `NAMED_INSIDE_AREA` is written twice. The later menu path ("Unit
///   inside an area.") and its shorter sentence replace the first ones.
/// - Mask 3 is set on five conditions (`CONDITION_FALSE`, `COUNTER`,
///   `COUNTER_COUNTER`, `FLAG`, `CONDITION_TRUE`); the only condition with
///   mask 2 is `LIVING_WORLD_IS_REGION_REF_BOUND`. All others, including
///   `TIMER_EXPIRED`, keep 1.
///
/// The body also contains a few assignments that the compiler removes. They
/// stand in for code the retail compile carried, so that the compiler makes
/// the same inlining choices as retail; they carry no meaning.
///
/// \par Modding
/// - Do not renumber slots: ScriptEngine::evaluateCondition handles
///   `CONDITION_FALSE`, `CONDITION_TRUE`, `COUNTER`, `FLAG` and
///   `TIMER_EXPIRED` by fixed type number and passes the rest to
///   `ScriptConditions`, which in Zero Hour also switches on the number.
///   WorldBuilder carries its own copy of the table. Map loading itself
///   re-matches a condition by internal name when its stored number
///   disagrees (Condition::ParseConditionDataChunk). The table holds 202
///   condition slots.
/// - Conditions are evaluated inside the deterministic game logic on every
///   machine. A condition whose mode mask excludes the current mode counts
///   as false, so its whole AND-term fails (\ref sub_scripting).
///
/// \par Provenance
/// The donor is BFME 1's separate condition-table function (Open-BFME-1
/// `game/GameEngine/Source/GameLogic/ScriptEngine/script_conditions.cpp`).
/// Every BFME 1 statement reappears in BFME 2 in the same order, and BFME 2
/// adds 18 types and the mode mask, read from the retail stores. No retail
/// symbol names this function; `initConditionTemplates` is a descriptive
/// name. Zero Hour fills its condition table inline in ScriptEngine::init.
///
/// \see ScriptEngine::initActionTemplates,
///      ScriptEngine::initTemplateNameKeys,
///      ScriptEngine::getConditionTemplate, Condition::setConditionType,
///      Condition::ParseConditionDataChunk, ScriptEngine::evaluateCondition
